\title{SIRUP: Spatial Immune Receptor Utilities Package}
\description{
SIRUP analyzes B cell and T cell receptor sequences from long-read spatial
sequencing. It turns demultiplexed reads into standard-formatted AIRR data with
spatial IDs, joins the AIRR data to Seurat spatial data, and includes tools for
examining and analyzing clones, lineage trees, tissue regions, and more in \emph{space}.
}
\details{
SIRUP takes demultiplexed reads from the companion RRAD package. The main
steps are:
\enumerate{
\item Process the reads. \code{\link[=processSpatialSample]{processSpatialSample()}} intakes demultiplexed reads
and writes an AIRR table.
\item Build a SIRUP object. \code{\link[=createSIRUP]{createSIRUP()}} joins a Seurat object and the
AIRR table. Use \code{\link[=saveSIRUP]{saveSIRUP()}} and \code{\link[=readSIRUP]{readSIRUP()}} to keep the object in
a file.
\item Draw and compare tissue regions. \code{\link[=selectSpatialPolygons]{selectSpatialPolygons()}} and
\code{\link[=addPolygons]{addPolygons()}} store polygons. \code{\link[=spatialCloneDominance]{spatialCloneDominance()}} and
\code{\link[=polygonDiversity]{polygonDiversity()}} summarize the receptors in each polygon.
\item Test spatial patterns. \code{\link[=spatialMoran]{spatialMoran()}}, \code{\link[=spatialCloneJoinCount]{spatialCloneJoinCount()}},
and \code{\link[=spatialRipleyCrossK]{spatialRipleyCrossK()}} test if genes or clones cluster in space.
\item Work with AIRR data as you typically would using \code{\link[=updateReceptors]{updateReceptors()}}.
Do the same with the spatial gene expression data with \code{\link[=updateSpatial]{updateSpatial()}}.
Then work with both together.
}
The package vignette gives a short introduction: \code{vignette("sirup", package = "SIRUP")}.
The documentation site has a tutorial for each step, guides, and the function
reference: \url{https://sirup.readthedocs.io}. To use SIRUP with an LLM agent, run
\code{\link[=installSIRUPSkill]{installSIRUPSkill()}}.
